    stimulus:
      'Police chief Rivera announced: "Violent crime in Greenville rose 50 percent last year, while it fell 5 percent statewide. Clearly, Greenville has become far more dangerous than the rest of the state." In fact, Greenville recorded 3 violent crimes last year, up from 2 the year before.',
    stem: 'Chief Rivera\'s reasoning is flawed because it',
    choices: [
      { text: 'compares Greenville\'s crime trend with the statewide trend without evidence the two are related' },
      { text: 'treats a large percentage increase as significant, ignoring the absolute numbers' },
      { text: 'assumes that past crime trends will continue into the future' },
      { text: 'fails to consider that some violent crimes may go unreported' },
      { text: 'draws a conclusion about danger from statistics about violent crime alone' },
    ],
    correctIndex: 1,
    explanationQuick:
      'A 50 percent rise sounds dramatic, but it represents a single additional crime — from 2 to 3. Percentages without base numbers mislead: the "surge" is trivial in absolute terms, so it cannot support the sweeping claim that Greenville is now far more dangerous than the state.',
    explanationWalkthrough:
      'The argument\'s force comes entirely from the contrast between "rose 50 percent" and "fell 5 percent." But percentages are meaningless without the underlying counts. Greenville went from 2 crimes to 3 — one additional incident, plausibly random fluctuation in a tiny base. The statewide 5 percent drop, by contrast, could represent thousands of fewer crimes. Comparing the two percentages as if they described comparable changes is the numbers flaw: it treats relative change as significant while ignoring absolute magnitude. With only 3 total crimes, Greenville may well be among the safest places in the state, not "far more dangerous." Choice (A) misses the point — the comparison itself is fine; the flaw is what the percentages conceal. Choice (C) invents a prediction about the future that Rivera never makes; her claim is about the present. Choice (D) raises underreporting, which would affect both figures and does not touch the percentage-versus-absolute error. Choice (E) quibbles about the scope of "danger," but violent crime is a perfectly reasonable proxy — the problem is the math, not the metric.',
    choiceExplanations: [
      'Comparing a town with its state is a legitimate move; the flaw is not the comparison but what the percentages hide. This choice mislocates the error, blaming the act of comparing instead of the failure to look behind the percentages at the absolute numbers.',
      'Correct. "Up 50 percent" is one additional crime (2 to 3). The dramatic percentage masks a trivial absolute change, and the conclusion that Greenville is "far more dangerous" collapses once the real numbers are visible. This is the classic relative-versus-absolute numbers flaw.',
      'Rivera makes no claim about what will happen next year; her conclusion concerns how dangerous Greenville has become. Attributing a future prediction to her is the unsupported trap — it criticizes a claim she never made.',
      'Underreporting is a genuine issue in crime statistics, but it applies to both Greenville and the statewide figures and does nothing to explain the specific error here. The flaw is visible even with perfect reporting: 2 to 3 is still just one more crime.',
      'Violent crime is a reasonable measure of dangerousness, so this scope quibble fails. The argument\'s defect is not its choice of metric but its treatment of a 50 percent increase on a base of 2 as though it were a meaningful surge.',
    ],
    generalLesson:
      'Percentages describe change relative to a starting point — always ask what that starting point was. A huge percentage of a tiny number can still be a tiny number.',
