    explanationQuick:
      'Sixty percent of residents is not sixty percent of voters. If only 40 percent turn out, the levy\'s fate depends on who shows up — supporters could stay home while opponents mobilize. The argument slides between two different reference classes (all residents vs. actual voters) as if they were the same group.',
    explanationWalkthrough:
      'This is a reference-class scope shift, and it is easy to miss because both figures are percentages about the same town. Unpack them. "Sixty percent of residents support the levy" describes all residents — voters and nonvoters alike. But elections are decided by voters, and only about 40 percent of residents typically vote. The argument needs the 60 percent figure to describe the electorate; instead it describes a larger, different group. If supporters are disproportionately among the 60 percent who stay home, the levy could fail decisively despite "broad support." The conclusion "certain to pass" therefore rests on silently substituting one group for another. Choice (B) is the tempting near-miss: it cries "unrepresentative sample," but the stimulus gives no reason to doubt the poll\'s sampling — the flaw survives even a perfect poll of all residents. Choice (C) quibbles about turnout matching history, but the flaw does not depend on the exact turnout figure; any turnout below 100 percent creates the gap. Choice (D) misdescribes the error as merely confusing two percentages rather than substituting reference classes. Choice (E) raises mind-changing, a real but secondary possibility that leaves the structural flaw untouched.',
    choiceExplanations: [
      'Correct. The 60 percent figure covers every resident, but only voters decide the election — and historically just 40 percent vote. The argument needs support among voters and offers support among residents instead, sliding between two different reference classes.',
      'This is the most tempting distractor, and the adversarial check matters here: the stimulus never suggests the poll was badly sampled. Even a flawless census of all residents would leave the argument broken, because the broken step is the leap from residents to voters, not the poll\'s methodology.',
      'Turnout might indeed differ from the historical average, but the flaw does not hinge on that. Whether turnout is 40 percent or 70 percent, "residents" and "voters" remain different groups, and the argument still substitutes one for the other.',
      'The campaign is not confusing the two percentages with each other; it is using the wrong group\'s percentage to predict an election outcome. This choice describes a surface mix-up rather than the structural substitution of reference classes.',
      'Supporters could change their minds, but that possibility is secondary. The argument fails even if every supporter\'s opinion is rock solid, because opinions of nonvoters do not cast ballots. This choice mistakes a side risk for the central flaw.',
    ],
    generalLesson:
      'A percentage is meaningless until you ask "percent of what?" When an argument moves between groups — residents vs. voters, customers vs. respondents — check that the figure it cites actually describes the group its conclusion is about.',
    misconceptionTags: ['scope-shift', 'reference-class', 'sampling-vs-scope'],
