    correctIndex: 4,
    explanationQuick:
      'If only app users could answer a survey about the app, the 80 percent satisfaction figure describes a self-selected group of users — not the membership. Non-users, who are likelier to be dissatisfied or indifferent, were structurally excluded, so the sample cannot support a claim about "most members."',
    explanationWalkthrough:
      'The conclusion is about all members; the evidence is from survey respondents. For the inference to work, respondents must represent the membership. Choice (E) shows they cannot: the invitation went out through the app itself, so the sample consists overwhelmingly of people who already use the app — the very group most likely to be satisfied with it. Members who tried the app and quit, or never downloaded it, never had a chance to answer. This is selection bias: the survey mechanism filters for the outcome it measures. An 80 percent satisfaction rate among self-selected users says nothing about the silent majority. Choice (A) is too weak — "some" members without smartphones does not show the sample was unrepresentative. Choice (B) is irrelevant: competitors\' apps do not affect PeakFit members\' opinions. Choice (C) is a methodological quibble; online surveys can be perfectly representative. Choice (D) is irrelevant-true: recommendation behavior is a different question from app satisfaction.',
    choiceExplanations: [
      '"Some" members without smartphones is too vague to damage the argument — it does not show the respondents were unrepresentative or that the 80 percent figure is misleading. Too-weak.',
      'What competing gyms offer has no bearing on whether PeakFit\'s members like its app. This choice changes the subject entirely — irrelevant. Members judge the app they use, not the market; rival offerings could be better or worse without changing a single member\'s satisfaction.',
      'The medium of a survey does not by itself bias the results. Without a reason online respondents differ from members generally, this is an empty methodological complaint — too-weak.',
      'Recommendation behavior is a different question from app satisfaction. This choice is irrelevant-true: it may be accurate about referrals while saying nothing about whether the survey sample represents the membership.',
      'Correct. Sending the invitation through the app guarantees the respondents are disproportionately app users — the population most likely to be satisfied. The survey systematically excludes non-users, so its results cannot be generalized to "most members."',
    ],
    generalLesson:
      'Always ask how survey respondents were selected. If the recruitment method filters for the very trait being measured — like surveying app users about the app — the results describe the filter, not the population.',
