      'The study is observational with a confounded rollout: breakfast and tutoring began together for the same students. Choice (A) exploits that design flaw directly. Tutoring independently raises test scores, so the score gap between breakfast eaters and skippers may reflect who got tutoring rather than who got breakfast — indeed, if the breakfast group overlaps the tutored group, the entire effect could be tutoring\'s. The district\'s conclusion requires attributing the gain to breakfast, which is impossible when a second score-raising intervention coincides. Choice (B) strengthens by supplying the mechanism (alertness). Choice (C) is the tempting funding-source attack, but sponsorship does not falsify data; the confound in (A) attacks the inference itself and is strictly stronger. Choice (D) is too weak: "some" low-scoring breakfast eaters are compatible with breakfast helping on average. Choice (E) is irrelevant-true — broad grade coverage does not address the confounding.',
