      'Strip the argument to its mechanism: no synthetic pesticides → healthier produce. That mechanism only works if synthetic pesticides on conventional produce actually pose a health risk. Choice (B) asks exactly that. A "yes" — residues reach harmful levels — validates the mechanism and supports the conclusion. A "no" — residues are negligible — removes the only reason offered for the health claim, leaving it unsupported. No other choice is similarly decisive. Choice (A) concerns taste, a different question from health. Choice (C) concerns the environment, also a different question — the conclusion is about human health, not ecology. Choice (D) is the wrong-viewpoint trap: Rao\'s personal diet does not validate or refute her reasoning. Choice (E) concerns price, irrelevant to the health comparison.',
