      'Job type is the lurking confound: demanding, fast-paced jobs might both drive workers to coffee and generate more completed tasks. If the groups hold similar jobs, the confound is ruled out and coffee remains as the explanation; if not, job differences could explain the entire gap.',
    explanationWalkthrough:
      'Reyes compares two self-selected groups — coffee drinkers and abstainers — who may differ in many ways besides coffee. Choice (A) investigates the most structurally dangerous difference: job type. High-pressure roles (trading floors, newsrooms, emergency dispatch) both demand heavy coffee consumption and produce high task counts; if coffee drinkers cluster in such roles, the productivity gap reflects job demands, not caffeine. A "similar jobs" answer eliminates that rival explanation and strengthens the conclusion; a "different jobs" answer supplies a complete alternative explanation and weakens it. Choice (B) is irrelevant: addictiveness does not address productivity effects. Choice (C) is irrelevant: taste preferences do not explain task counts. Choice (D) is the wrong-viewpoint trap. Choice (E) is tempting but weaker: the decaf comparison tests caffeine specifically, yet even a decaf difference would still leave the job-type confound unaddressed — and the question asks for the most useful single question.',
    choiceExplanations: [
      'Correct. Job type is the leading confound: demanding jobs could cause both coffee drinking and high task completion. Whether the groups hold similar jobs directly determines whether the productivity gap can be credited to coffee.',
      'Addictiveness is irrelevant to the productivity claim. Coffee could be addictive and productivity-enhancing, or addictive and irrelevant — this question does not discriminate. Because addictiveness is compatible with both productivity gains and productivity losses, the answer cannot move the causal needle either way.',
      'Taste preferences have no bearing on task completion rates. This question is irrelevant to evaluating the causal claim. Enjoying coffee\'s taste is an aesthetic matter; it cannot confirm or disconfirm whether caffeine changes how much work gets done.',
      'Reyes\'s personal habits are irrelevant to whether her reasoning is sound. This is the wrong-viewpoint trap. Whether Reyes drinks coffee herself tells you about her routine, not her reasoning; the claim must be judged on its evidence.',
      'The decaf comparison is somewhat informative about caffeine, but it leaves the job-type confound untouched: even decaf drinkers might hold different jobs. It is useful but strictly less diagnostic than the correct choice — half-right.',
    ],
    generalLesson:
      'In group comparisons, the most useful question targets the most plausible pre-existing difference between the groups. Rule out the leading confound first; finer mechanistic questions come second.',
