    correctIndex: 2,
    explanationQuick:
      'The prediction is an analogy: what worked in City X will work here. Analogies transfer only when the cases are relevantly similar — if our traffic patterns, cycling rates, and road layouts differ sharply from City X\'s, the 35 percent result may not travel. Comparability is the load-bearing question.',
    explanationWalkthrough:
      'The council\'s inference has the form: intervention I produced outcome O in case X; therefore I will produce O in case Y. That inference is only as strong as the relevant similarity between X and Y. Choice (C) tests exactly that: traffic patterns (speeds, congestion, truck traffic) and cycling rates (how many cyclists, how experienced) are the very factors that determine whether protected lanes cut injuries. A "similar" answer licenses the transfer; a "dissimilar" answer — say, our city has far faster arterial traffic and almost no existing cyclists — breaks the analogy and the prediction with it. Choice (A) is irrelevant: construction cost does not affect injury outcomes. Choice (B) is the wrong-viewpoint trap: cyclist enthusiasm does not show the lanes will work. Choice (D) is too weak: a recent injury trend says nothing about whether our city resembles City X. Choice (E) is irrelevant political trivia.',
    choiceExplanations: [
      'Construction costs are irrelevant to whether the lanes would reduce injuries. This question concerns budgets, not the analogy\'s validity. Expensive infrastructure can be effective and cheap infrastructure useless; cost says nothing about whether City X\'s experience will transfer.',
      'Cyclists\' support is politically relevant but evidentially inert: enthusiasm does not show the intervention will transfer. Wrong-viewpoint. Supporters can be passionate about an intervention that fails elsewhere; enthusiasm measures politics, while the question demands evidence about injuries.',
      'Correct. Relevant similarity is what makes analogical predictions work. Traffic patterns and cycling rates are the factors that determine whether City X\'s result transfers — the answer directly controls the prediction\'s credibility.',
      'A recent injury trend in our city does not address whether our city is relevantly similar to City X. Too weak to evaluate the analogical transfer.',
      'The mayor\'s electoral fortunes are irrelevant to whether bike lanes cut injuries or whether our city resembles City X. Pure trivia. Election prospects concern the mayor\'s career, not the analogy\'s validity; they cannot confirm or disconfirm the causal transfer.',
    ],
