    correctIndex: 1,
    explanationQuick:
      'The conclusion\'s mechanism requires the checklist to have actually changed behavior in operating rooms. If compliance was high, the checklist is a live candidate for the 20 percent drop; if teams largely ignored it, the checklist cannot explain the improvement and some other cause must be responsible. Compliance is the gatekeeper question.',
    explanationWalkthrough:
      'This item is difficult because the distractors gesture at real considerations while missing the argument\'s load-bearing assumption. The director\'s causal story is: checklist → fewer errors → fewer complications. That story presupposes the checklist was actually used. Choice (B) tests that presupposition directly, and it is maximally diagnostic: high compliance keeps the causal claim alive (the checklist could have done the work), while low compliance kills it outright (an unused checklist cannot reduce errors, so the 20 percent drop must have another cause — perhaps a Hawthorne effect from being observed, or staffing changes). No other choice has this gatekeeper property. Choice (A) shifts scope to other hospitals, which cannot show what happened at Memorial. Choice (C) concerns cost, a different question from efficacy. Choice (D) is the wrong-viewpoint trap. Choice (E) is irrelevant-true: complication costs do not bear on what caused the decline.',
    choiceExplanations: [
      'Other hospitals\' practices cannot establish what caused Memorial\'s decline. This choice shifts scope away from the hospital in question — scope-shift. What other hospitals do is a different data set; the claim is about Memorial\'s complications, which only Memorial\'s evidence can explain.',
      'Correct. Compliance is the gatekeeper: an unused checklist cannot reduce errors. High compliance preserves the causal claim; low compliance refutes it outright. The answer directly determines the conclusion\'s viability.',
      'Implementation costs are irrelevant to whether the checklist caused the complication drop. This question answers a budget question, not the causal one. A costly checklist can still be the true cause of the decline, and a cheap one can be irrelevant; cost does not test causation.',
      'The director\'s awards are biographical, not evidential. This is the wrong-viewpoint trap: it evaluates the claimant\'s résumé instead of the claim\'s mechanism. Decorated administrators can advance flawed causal claims; the complication decline must be explained by evidence, not by credentials.',
      'That complications are costly is true but inert: it explains why the hospital cares, not what caused the 20 percent decline. Irrelevant-true. High costs motivate the search for solutions but never identify the true cause; expensive problems can have unrelated explanations.',
    ],
    generalLesson:
      'Causal claims about an intervention presuppose the intervention was actually implemented. Compliance is the gatekeeper question: if the treatment was not delivered, it cannot have caused the effect.',
